{; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ; ONE FAMILY. `:seon.fault/*` merged in here 2026-07-27 (orchestrator
 ; ruling) BEFORE anything referenced those names: an error is an error
 ; whether it arrived as a flat value an agent reads, a transition's
 ; refusal, or a Throwable that escaped our own code onto flow's error
 ; channel. Two shapes in one family, and the relation between them is
 ; a projection, not a conversion:
 ;
 ;   :seon.error/value — the FLAT value. Nothing throws into the run
 ;     loop, so this is what a caller branches on and what an agent is
 ;     shown. Producers keep their own closed maps under `data`.
 ;   :seon.error/fact  — the DURABLE fact. One committed entity per
 ;     error worth remembering, carrying the projected evidence and
 ;     refs to what it interrupted. `seon.error/value` projects a fact
 ;     back down to the flat shape, which is why the totality property
 ;     asserts both of one normalization.
 ;
 ; KINDS ARE NEVER ENUMERATED HERE. `:seon.error/kind` stays `:keyword`
 ; on purpose: the kind population is the set of namespaced keywords the
 ; producing sites already carry (`::no-credential`, `:seon.db/rejected`,
 ; `:seon.cluster.run/refused`, …). A `[:enum]` here would be a
 ; hand-maintained list of everyone else's vocabulary and would go stale
 ; the first time a site is added. N5's program graph makes the real
 ; enumeration QUERYABLE from the corpus; until then it is discoverable
 ; by reading the producers, and neither answer is a list in this file.
 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

 ; --- the flat value -------------------------------------------------------
 ; `kind` is the namespaced rule that failed, `message` is what a reader
 ; (agent or human) is told, and `data` carries whatever the owner of
 ; that rule attached. Producers stay closed maps of their own; this is
 ; the shared floor.
 :seon.error/data :map
 :seon.error/kind :keyword
 :seon.error/message [:string {:min 1}]
 :seon.error/value [:map {:closed true} [:seon.error/kind :seon.error/kind] [:seon.error/message :seon.error/message] [:seon.error/data {:optional true} :seon.error/data]]

 ; --- the durable fact -----------------------------------------------------
 ; Classification is the CHANNEL, not a predicate — `seon.sci.eval/evaluate`
 ; never throws, so an agent's mistake is a VALUE and can only become a
 ; receipt; anything arriving on ::flow/error is definitionally ours. No
 ; kind list, no ambient scope, no authorship inference.
 :seon.error/id [:string {:min 1 :seon.db/identity true}]
 :seon.error/at :inst
 ; the ONE holder string, `seon.cluster/process-identity`
 :seon.error/process [:string {:min 1}]
 :seon.error/proc :keyword
 :seon.error/op :keyword
 :seon.error/cid :keyword
 :seon.error/class [:string {:min 1}]
 :seon.error/basis-t [:int {:min 0}]
 ; the projected source, printed. NEVER a raw pr-str: two of flow's three
 ; report shapes carry ::flow/state, which for the run loop holds a live
 ; Datahike connection and executors. One codec — `seon.sci.admit/admit`.
 :seon.error/data-edn :string
 ; the honest signal that `data-edn` is not the whole source, so a reader
 ; never has to guess whether an elision marker was the original value
 :seon.error/capped? :boolean
 ; a derivation of the error's OWN content, committed with it — never a
 ; mutable tally. Recurrence is counted by query. Deliberately excludes
 ; the message: a message carrying an id or a timestamp would make every
 ; occurrence unique and recurrence undetectable.
 :seon.error/signature [:re {:seon.db/index true} "^[0-9a-f]{64}$"]
 :seon.error/agent [:and {:seon.db/index true} :seon.db/ref]
 :seon.error/run [:and {:seon.db/index true} :seon.db/ref]

 ; The entity map is not decoration: canonical-database-attributes
 ; installs entity-map entries BY CONSTRUCTION, and a family declared
 ; only as standalone leaves installs nothing but its identity —
 ; the fixture-vs-live-boot class that cost the N3 drive two rounds.
 :seon.error/fact
 [:map {:seon.db/entity true}
  [:seon.error/id :seon.error/id]
  [:seon.error/at :seon.error/at]
  [:seon.error/process :seon.error/process]
  ; REQUIRED, where the merged draft had it optional: normalization is
  ; fail-closed, so a source carrying no discoverable kind gets
  ; `:seon.error/unclassified` rather than an absent key. That is what
  ; makes "every fact projects to a valid flat value" true by
  ; construction, and it means a kind query can never silently miss.
  [:seon.error/kind :seon.error/kind]
  [:seon.error/message :seon.error/message]
  [:seon.error/signature :seon.error/signature]
  [:seon.error/data-edn :seon.error/data-edn]
  [:seon.error/capped? :seon.error/capped?]
  ; absent when the error was never a Throwable — a flat value and a
  ; transition refusal have no class, and absence is the state
  [:seon.error/class {:optional true} :seon.error/class]
  ; absent for everything that did not arrive on flow's error channel,
  ; and `op`/`cid` are absent for two of its three report shapes
  [:seon.error/proc {:optional true} :seon.error/proc]
  [:seon.error/op {:optional true} :seon.error/op]
  [:seon.error/cid {:optional true} :seon.error/cid]
  ; absent when no database value was pinned at the error
  [:seon.error/basis-t {:optional true} :seon.error/basis-t]
  ; absent when no claimed-open run existed at the error's basis
  [:seon.error/run {:optional true} :seon.error/run]
  [:seon.error/agent {:optional true} :seon.error/agent]]

 ; --- normalization --------------------------------------------------------
 ; `:any` at the ONE genuine third-party boundary of this family: the
 ; source is whatever went wrong — a Throwable from any library, one of
 ; core.async.flow's three incompatible report maps, a flat value, or a
 ; transition's ex-data. A schema here would be a second classifier, and
 ; the whole point of one normalizer is that there is no second one.
 :seon.error/source :any
 ; NOTE what is NOT a key here: `:seon.config/on-core-error`. The error
 ; path may not panic — a panic while recording an error destroys the
 ; one record of the original failure (the quarry's recursion fence,
 ; src-old/seon/error.cljc:738-745). Admission is therefore called in
 ; `:record` mode unconditionally, and a codec hole stays visible as a
 ; projection marker plus `capped?` rather than as a throw.
 :seon.error/normalize-request [:map {:closed true} [:seon.error/source :seon.error/source] [:seon.error/id :seon.error/id] [:seon.error/at :seon.error/at] [:seon.error/process :seon.error/process] [:seon.sci.admit/caps :seon.sci.admit/caps] [:seon.error/basis-t {:optional true} :seon.error/basis-t] [:seon.cluster.run/id {:optional true} :seon.cluster.run/id] [:seon.cluster.agent/id {:optional true} :seon.cluster.agent/id]]

 ; --- the notice: the derived routing unit ---------------------------------
 ; WHY an agent is being told. A derived clause, never boilerplate — and
 ; the reason IS the escalation rule, so the two cannot drift. It is not
 ; an attribute of the fact because it is per-RECIPIENT: one fact can be
 ; :your-run to the interrupted agent and :recurring to the escalation
 ; owner in the same transaction.
 :seon.error/reason [:enum :your-run :no-attributable-agent :recurring]
 :seon.error/notice-request [:map {:closed true} [:seon.error/fact :seon.error/fact] [:seon.error/reason {:optional true} :seon.error/reason] [:seon.cluster.agent/id {:optional true} :seon.cluster.agent/id]]
 ; The notice is DERIVED and never stored: a fact plus the projection
 ; declarations, which is what makes it routable through the one generic
 ; router (`seon.render`). The projection keys ride on the notice rather
 ; than on the entity because a stored symbol identical on every row is
 ; stored-derived data, and because the ai projection needs the reason
 ; the entity cannot carry.
 :seon.error/notice [:map {:closed true} [:seon.error/fact :seon.error/fact] [:seon.render/ai :seon.render/projection] [:seon.render/log :seon.render/projection] [:seon.error/reason {:optional true} :seon.error/reason] [:seon.cluster.agent/id {:optional true} :seon.cluster.agent/id]]

 ; --- step 2's commit, renamed here, NOT drafted here ----------------------
 ; `seon.error/commit!` (the boot-wired fault committer, D4) is the next
 ; slice's owner; these two shapes landed with slice 1 and are renamed in
 ; place so that nothing ever references `:seon.fault/*`. Its author
 ; revises them against the normalizer above.
 :seon.error/messages [:int {:min 0}]
 :seon.error/commit-request [:map {:closed true} [:seon.store/branch-connection :seon.store/branch-connection] [:seon.error/source :seon.error/source] [:seon.error/process :seon.error/process] [:seon.sci.admit/caps :seon.sci.admit/caps] [:seon.config/on-core-error :seon.config/on-core-error] [:seon.config.fault/escalate-to {:optional true} :seon.config.fault/escalate-to] [:seon.config.fault/recurrence-limit {:optional true} :seon.config.fault/recurrence-limit]]
 :seon.error/committed [:map {:closed true} [:seon.error/id :seon.error/id] [:seon.error/messages :seon.error/messages] [:seon.error/reason {:optional true} :seon.error/reason]]

 ; An explanation message carries a ref to what it explains. A user
 ; message has none — absence is the state, and it is what makes the
 ; storm fence computable without a flag. The `:and` wrap is the one
 ; idiom for a facet on a referenced form, and the facet is not
 ; decoration: a BARE `:seon.db/ref` carries no persistence facet and
 ; would never install as a canonical database attribute (the computed
 ; rule provenance.edn documents). Slice 1 declared it bare, so a
 ; fault-explanation message would have been untransactable on the live
 ; boot path and no fixture would have seen it. Indexed because the
 ; storm fence and "which messages explain this error" both query BY
 ; the ref.
 :seon.cluster.message/about [:and {:seon.db/index true} :seon.db/ref]}
